---------------------------------------------------------------------------
SOS basis layer — next steps
---------------------------------------------------------------------------

* FIXED: Compose + domain-multiplier combined with Hermite/
  HermiteNormalized (test/test_compose_multiplier.ml)

  Putinar-style certificates that combine Compose with a domain-multiplier
  product (v(f(x)) - lambda(x)*s(x) must be SOS, the shape used by
  build_positivity_constraint / surapproximate_image in the saimcop
  project) used to solve fine with Classical while Hermite and
  HermiteNormalized reported PrimalInfeasible on the exact same
  constraints. Since SOS-feasibility at a fixed Gram-matrix dimension
  cannot depend on which basis is used to search for the certificate,
  this had to be a numerical artifact, not a real infeasibility (Classical
  succeeding on the identical problem was the witness).

  Isolated repro in test/test_compose_multiplier.ml (part 2); part 1
  confirms Compose alone and a multiplier product alone each work fine in
  every basis, so the bug was specific to the combination.

  Root cause, confirmed (not just hypothesised): Classical.refine_basis
  delegates to NewtonPolytope.filter, pruning candidate Gram-matrix
  elements down to those that can actually contribute to the target's
  monomial support; Hermite/HermiteNormalized's refine_basis used to be
  the identity (no pruning), so for the same nominal degree their Gram
  matrix was considerably larger/less pruned than Classical's (measured:
  4 vs 6 elements on the test_compose_multiplier repro). Confirmed by
  forcing Hermite onto Classical's pruned candidate set via
  options.monoms, which flipped the same PrimalInfeasible into
  Success/PartialSuccess; forcing it onto the full unpruned set reproduced
  the failure. Fix applied: Hermite.refine_basis now also delegates to
  NewtonPolytope.filter (HermiteNormalized inherits it unchanged).

  Caveat: NewtonPolytope.filter's criterion (2*index in the convex hull of
  the target support) is derived from monomial semantics (a single
  reachable exponent per product pair) and is not a priori a tight, or
  even proven-sound, necessary condition for Hermite indices, whose
  products reach a whole range of degrees (|a-b| .. a+b, matching parity)
  rather than a single exponent. It works on every case tested so far
  (test_compose_multiplier.ml, the saimcop project's examples), but a
  fully rigorous Hermite-specific reachability analysis remains future
  work -- watch for cases where this heuristic might not be tight enough
  (leaving avoidable size in the Gram matrix) or, worse, incorrectly
  sound (pruning something Hermite genuinely needs).

* Adding more orthogonal polynomial bases

  The BASIS interface in sosBasis.mli is already fully abstracted: each
  basis only needs to implement to_classical, prod_to_classical, list_le,
  list_eq, nth, and refine_basis.  The multivariate case is handled
  generically via TensorProduct, so adding a univariate basis automatically
  gives the full multivariate one.

  Candidate bases to add:

  - Chebyshev (1st kind, T_n): good conditioning on [-1,1]^n; recurrence
    T_0=1, T_1=x, T_{n+1}=2x T_n - T_{n-1}.  prod_to_classical via the
    identity T_m T_n = (T_{m+n} + T_{|m-n|})/2.

  - Chebyshev (2nd kind, U_n): related to trigonometric moments; U_0=1,
    U_1=2x, U_{n+1}=2x U_n - U_{n-1}.  Useful for integration weights.

  - Legendre (P_n): orthogonal on [-1,1] w.r.t. Lebesgue measure;
    (n+1)P_{n+1}=(2n+1)x P_n - n P_{n-1}.  Natural for polynomials on
    bounded domains without the endpoint singularity of Chebyshev.

  - Laguerre (L_n): orthogonal on [0,∞) w.r.t. e^{-x}; useful for SOS
    on half-spaces or positive-orthant constraints without lifting to a
    compact domain.

  For each new basis, the work is:
  1. Implement the univariate recurrence and the closed-form product rule
     (or compute it via to_classical + classical multiplication).
  2. Plug into Registry so the basis_choice type and the dispatch in
     sos.ml pick it up automatically.
  3. Add unit tests in test_sosbasis.ml: to_classical sampling,
     prod_to_classical sampling, enumeration sizes.
  4. Add residual tests in test_residuals.ml: solve a few SOS problems
     in the new basis, check coefficient residuals.

  Note: for bases that are not orthonormal, the conditioning of the Gram
  matrix differs.  The pad_list mechanism already compensates for some of
  this, but it may be worth tuning default padding per basis.


* Interpolation-based encoding of the Gram-matrix linear dependencies

  The current build_cstr function (sos.ml) encodes the SOS-to-LMI reduction
  by matching polynomial coefficients: for each classical monomial m it
  produces one linear equality

    sum_{(i,j): prod(v_i,v_j) has monomial m} Q_{ij} = coeff(p, m).

  An alternative is the interpolation (or discretization) approach:
  choose a set of evaluation points {x_1,...,x_N} (N >= dim of basis vector)
  and encode the SOS constraint as

    v(x_k)^T Q v(x_k) = p(x_k)   for k=1,...,N

  where v(x_k) is the vector of basis values at x_k.  This yields the same
  LMI once N is large enough (a Vandermonde-type argument), but the matrix
  of coefficients V with V_{ki} = basis_i(x_k) is often much better
  conditioned than the monomial-matching system, especially for Chebyshev
  or Hermite bases with their natural quadrature points.

  Implementation plan:

  - Add val interpolation_points : int -> int -> float list list to the
    BASIS interface (returns N >= get_sos_size n d points for n variables
    and degree d).  For Chebyshev use Chebyshev-Lobatto nodes, for Hermite
    use Gauss-Hermite quadrature points, for Classical use equispaced or
    Leja points.

  - Add a new encoding mode (e.g., options.encoding = Monomial | Interpolation)
    in sos.mli.  In Interpolation mode, build_cstr is replaced by a function
    that evaluates the basis vector at each interpolation point and builds
    the Vandermonde constraints directly.

  - The float conversion (currently at the SDP boundary in float_block)
    remains valid: the basis evaluations are exact rationals (or exact for
    the algebraic nodes), converted to float only when passed to the SDP
    solver.

  - Expected benefit: better numerical conditioning for high-degree
    polynomials and for bases with fast-decaying coefficients (Chebyshev,
    Hermite), at the cost of slightly larger but denser LMI constraint
    matrices.


* Sampling-based encoding of the Gram-matrix linear dependencies

  Related to the interpolation-based encoding above (deterministic
  evaluation points), a further alternative is a genuinely random
  sampling-based construction: draw N sample points {x_1,...,x_N} from a
  probability distribution matched to the chosen basis's orthogonality
  measure, and build the SOS constraint from the sampled system

    v(x_k)^T Q v(x_k) = p(x_k)   for k=1,...,N

  exactly as in the interpolation approach, but with x_k drawn i.i.d.
  rather than placed at fixed quadrature nodes. This trades the
  determinism/tightness of quadrature nodes for scalability to higher
  dimensions (tensor-product quadrature node counts grow badly with the
  number of variables, while i.i.d. sampling does not).

  The sampling distribution should follow the basis's own weight
  function so that the samples are "typical" for that basis:

  - Classical (monomial) basis: no canonical weight -- uniform (or an
    arcsine-type distribution on [-1,1]^n) is a reasonable default.
  - Hermite / HermiteNormalized: Gaussian N(0,1)^n, matching the weight
    e^{-x^2} the Hermite polynomials are orthogonal against.
  - Chebyshev (1st kind, once added): arcsine distribution on [-1,1]
    (density 1/(pi*sqrt(1-x^2))), matching the Chebyshev weight.
  - Legendre (once added): uniform on [-1,1].
  - Laguerre (once added): Exponential(1) on [0,infty), matching e^{-x}.

  Implementation plan:

  - Add val sample_distribution : unit -> (unit -> float) to the BASIS
    interface (or a registry keyed on basis_choice), returning a sampler
    for the basis's natural univariate measure; multivariate samples are
    then n independent draws (tensor-product measure), consistent with
    how TensorProduct already builds the multivariate basis from
    univariate ones.
  - Extend options.encoding (see interpolation-based encoding above) with
    a Sampling variant carrying the sample count N and an optional seed
    for reproducibility.
  - Reuse the same constraint-building code path as the interpolation
    mode; only the choice of {x_k} differs.
  - Needs a rank/conditioning check after sampling (or a resampling
    retry): unlike quadrature nodes, there is no a priori guarantee that
    N random points give a full-rank system.


* Symbolic encoding and possible uses

  The SOS-to-LMI reduction currently stays exact (in P.Coeff.t, typically
  rational) up to the float_block call at the SDP boundary.  This structure
  already supports symbolic instantiation: replacing Scalar.Q with a
  Scalar.Symbolic module (whose t is an expression AST) and bypassing
  float_block would produce the LMI constraints as closed-form symbolic
  expressions rather than float matrices.

  Concretely: introduce a separate entry point

    val encode : options -> polynomial_expr list -> symbolic_sdp

  that runs the full build_cstr / padding pipeline but stops before
  float_block, returning the constraint matrices as (Monomial.t * Coeff.t)
  expressions.

  Possible uses:

  - Formal verification certificates: the symbolic LMI can be exported to
    a proof assistant (Coq/Lean) and the certificate checked inside the
    prover using exact arithmetic, without trusting the SDP solver output.

  - Parametric SOS: if the polynomial p contains symbolic parameters
    (e.g., p = x^4 + a*x^2 + b), encode produces the LMI as a function
    of (a, b).  Sweeping over parameter values then only requires
    re-evaluating the float_block step, not re-running the full encoding.

  - Code generation: the symbolic SDP can be compiled to efficient C or
    Fortran code for embedded or online solvers where the problem structure
    is fixed but the data changes at runtime.

  - SMT-LIB / interval arithmetic export: route the symbolic constraints
    to an interval arithmetic checker or an SMT solver to get a
    machine-verified bound without any floating-point rounding errors.


* Export the generated SDP problem in an open format

  Currently the constraint matrices built by build_cstr / float_block are
  handed directly, in-process, to whichever solver backend is selected
  (Csdp, Sdpa, Mosek, ...); there is no way to inspect or reuse the
  generated SDP outside of osdp itself.

  Add an export path that serializes the final (post-padding,
  post-float_block) SDP -- the block-diagonal LMI data, the objective
  vector, and any linear equality/inequality constraints -- to a
  standard open format instead of (or in addition to) feeding it
  straight to a solver:

  - SDPA sparse format (.dat-s): the de facto text format understood by
    CSDP, SDPA, and most other SDP solvers; simplest to produce since
    osdp's internal block/sparse-matrix representation is already close
    to it.
  - Alternatively/additionally, the more modern CBF (Conic Benchmark
    Format) used by e.g. Mosek and the conic-benchmark library, which
    also supports mixing SDP cones with LP/SOCP cones and would be
    better suited if osdp ever mixes cone types.

  Suggested entry point:

    val export : format -> options -> polynomial_expr list -> out_channel -> unit

  where format = Sdpa | Cbf, running through the same build_cstr /
  padding pipeline used before calling a solver, but writing the
  resulting blocks to the given channel instead of invoking Csdp/Sdpa
  in-process.

  Benefits:

  - Reproducibility/debugging: a solver-independent problem instance can
    be replayed with a different solver or solver version without going
    back through osdp.
  - Benchmarking: generated problems can be fed to SDP benchmark suites
    (SDPLIB-style collections) or compared across solver versions.
  - Interop: problems built with osdp's polynomial/SOS frontend become
    usable from other tools that consume SDPA/CBF but have no SOS layer
    of their own.

  Independent of the encoding work above (Monomial / Interpolation /
  Sampling) -- whichever encoding produces the LMI blocks, the same
  export step can serialize the result.
